{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Let her not, I pray.</b> 'Our sister' — this is its meaning." "<b>As one dead.</b> For the metzora is accounted like the dead — as the dead defiles by entry [into a room], so the metzora defiles by entry (ibid.)." "<b>When he comes out of his mother's womb.</b> It should have said 'our mother,' but Scripture used a euphemism. And likewise 'half his flesh' — it should have said 'half our flesh,' but Scripture used a euphemism. Since she came out of the womb of our mother, she is to us as though half our flesh were eaten — as in the matter stated: ״כִּי אָחִינוּ בְשָׂרֵנוּ הוּא״ (Genesis 37). And according to its plain sense it also reads accordingly: it is not fitting for a brother to leave his sister to be as one dead." "<b>When he comes out.</b> Since this one came out of the womb of this one's mother — and this one has the power to help but does not help him — behold, <b>half his flesh</b> is eaten, for his brother is his flesh. Another interpretation: <b>Let her not be, I pray, as one dead</b> — if you do not heal her through prayer, who will deliver her, who will pronounce her clean? I am unable to examine her, for I am a close relative and a close relative may not examine nega'im, and there is no other priest in the world — and this is <b>when he comes out of his mother's womb</b>."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:10:39.025181927Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}